        "_id": "68ee3d43ac08e08e2e57a519",
        "title": "D365 Invoice Matching: A Guide",
        "description": "D365 Invoice Matching is a critical financial control mechanism within Microsoft Dynamics 365 (specifically \u003Ca href=\"https://plenum-tech.com/service/finance-operations\" target=\"_blank\"\u003EDynamics 365 Finance\u003C/a\u003E) used to ensure that vendor invoices are accurate and align with expected amounts before payment is processed. \r\nThis process helps prevent overpayments, unauthorized purchases, and fraudulent activities. It is an essential component of the Procure-to-Pay cycle, establishing a necessary verification step after goods or services have been received. \r\nBy automating this control, organizations using \u003Ca href=\"https://plenum-tech.com/service/microsoft-dynamics\" target=\"_blank\"\u003EMicrosoft Dynamics 365\u003C/a\u003E can significantly improve accounts payable efficiency and accuracy.\r\n",
        "metaDescription": "Master D365 Invoice Matching in Microsoft Dynamics 365. Learn about 2-way, 3-way, and N-way matching to ensure accurate financial control and efficiency.",
        "category": "Blog",
        "blogCategory": "plenum",
        "userRef": {
            "_id": "68661ad1a62859854fb1162f",
            "fullname": "Xntric",
            "email": "info@xntric.me"
        },
        "comments": [],
        "like": [],
        "imageURL": "http://res.cloudinary.com/dmvs9zlbt/image/upload/v1760443713/xk63wvpw1lennsudwhp2.jpg",
        "bannerImageURL": "http://res.cloudinary.com/dmvs9zlbt/image/upload/v1760443714/f0bdmmzwazjtfvap2q0w.jpg",
        "quotes": "",
        "conclusion": "",
        "subsections": [
            {
                "subtitle": "Understanding Invoice Matching in Microsoft Dynamics 365",
                "subdescription": [
                    "Invoice matching, often referred to as \u003Ca href=\"https://corporatefinanceinstitute.com/resources/accounting/purchase-order/\" target=\"_blank\"\u003EPurchase Order\u003C/a\u003E (PO) matching, is the practice of comparing the information on a vendor's invoice against corresponding internal documents. \nIn a comprehensive Enterprise Resource Planning (ERP) system like Microsoft Dynamics 365 ERP, this process uses predefined tolerances and rules to automatically approve or flag invoices for manual review. This automation streamlines the accounts payable (AP) workflow and reduces the risk of human error."
                ],
                "lists": [
                    {
                        "listTitle": "The Core Documents for Invoice Matching",
                        "listDescription": "The matching process in D365 primarily involves three documents:",
                        "items": [
                            {
                                "title": "Purchase Order (PO): The initial document created by the purchasing department, which details the items, quantities, and agreed-upon prices from the vendor.",
                                "description": ""
                            },
                            {
                                "title": "Product Receipt: An internal document confirming the quantity and date of goods or services actually received.",
                                "description": ""
                            },
                            {
                                "title": "Vendor Invoice: The external document from the vendor requesting payment, detailing the invoiced quantity and price.",
                                "description": ""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                "subtitle": "Types of D365 Invoice Matching",
                "subdescription": [
                    "D365 supports different matching policies to accommodate varying levels of control and risk. The most common types are 2-way and 3-way matching."
                ],
                "lists": [
                    {
                        "listTitle": "2-Way Invoice Matching: Price Verification",
                        "listDescription": "The 2-way invoice matching process primarily serves as a price verification mechanism by comparing the line-level price and quantity information on the vendor invoice directly against the corresponding details on the original Purchase Order (PO).  Its core purpose is to ensure that the invoiced unit price and total quantity do not exceed the amounts approved on the PO by more than a specified tolerance percentage or amount.  This matching method is often deployed for purchases of services, subscriptions, or low-value items where physically receiving and counting the goods (i.e., posting a product receipt) is either impractical or deemed unnecessary for internal control. Because it bypasses the product receipt, it offers a faster, simpler verification flow compared to 3-way matching.",
                        "items": []
                    },
                    {
                        "listTitle": "3-Way Invoice Matching: Comprehensive Control",
                        "listDescription": "The 2-way invoice matching process primarily serves as a price verification mechanism by comparing the line-level price and quantity information on the vendor invoice directly against the corresponding details on the original Purchase Order (PO). Its core purpose is to ensure that the invoiced unit price and total quantity do not exceed the amounts approved on the PO by more than a specified tolerance percentage or amount.  This matching method is often deployed for purchases of services, subscriptions, or low-value items where physically receiving and counting the goods (i.e., posting a product receipt) is either impractical or deemed unnecessary for internal control. Because it bypasses the product receipt, it offers a faster, simpler verification flow compared to 3-way matching.",
                        "items": []
                    },
                    {
                        "listTitle": "N-Way Matching: Beyond the Standard",
                        "listDescription": "In some specialized scenarios, D365 can support an N-way matching concept, which can include additional checks. For example, a 4-way match might incorporate a Quality Inspection Report or a Contract Agreement to verify compliance before processing payment. This level of customization can be crucial for organizations with complex regulatory or quality assurance requirements.",
                        "items": []
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
        "faqs": [
            {
                "question": "How to do invoice matching?",
                "answer": "Invoice matching in D365 is primarily done automatically. Users must first enable the matching policy (2-way or 3-way) on the purchase order or vendor parameters. When the invoice is entered or imported, the system automatically compares the invoice lines to the corresponding PO and Product Receipts. \nIf the discrepancies (variance) are within the set tolerance thresholds, the invoice passes matching. If not, the system flags the invoice with a matching error, requiring a manual review and approval by a designated AP manager or buyer."
            },
            {
                "question": "What is the 3-way invoice matching process?",
                "answer": "The 3-way invoice matching process validates three data points across three documents:\nInvoice quantity and price.\nPurchase Order (PO) quantity and price.\nProduct Receipt quantity.\nThe process ensures that the invoiced items match the ordered items (PO) and that the quantity billed is the same as the quantity physically received (Product Receipt)."
            },
            {
                "question": "What can you match invoices against?",
                "answer": "You can match invoices against:\nPurchase Orders (POs): To verify prices and ordered quantities.\nProduct Receipts: To verify received quantities.\nContracts or Agreements: In more complex setups to verify terms or specific item configurations (N-way matching)."
            },
            {
                "question": "How to match a payment to an invoice in MYOB?",
                "answer": "While this guide focuses on D365 Invoice Matching, in accounting software like MYOB, matching a payment to an invoice is typically done within the Bank Reconciliation or Receive Payments module. \nThe user selects the customer or vendor payment and then links it to the open invoice(s) it is intended to cover, effectively closing the outstanding balance. The term \"invoice matching\" in the context of PO validation is a separate process generally associated with an ERP system's Accounts Payable function."
            }
        ],
